<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Vitus Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1344–1929</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as St. Vitus Cathedral</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St Vitus Cathedral is the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a>. Begun in 1344 under Charles IV by Matthias of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> and continued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-parler">Peter Parler</a>, it was left unfinished during the Hussite Wars and completed only in 1929. It holds the tombs of Bohemian kings and saints, including St Wenceslas, and the crown jewels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> are kept there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-vitus-cathedral.jpg" alt="St Vitus Cathedral" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MathKnight and Zachi Evenor · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PragueCathedral03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
